Accept padded ChannelData in the TURN client.

RFC 8656 section 12.5 lets a TURN server pad a ChannelData message to a
multiple of four bytes, and requires it over TCP. eturnal pads over UDP as
well. ex_turn 0.2.3 matched `data::binary-size(len)` against the whole
datagram, so any payload whose length is not a multiple of four arrived with
one to three trailing bytes, failed the match, and was dropped as "invalid
channel data".

That breaks WebRTC media relayed through TURN. Chrome's DTLS ClientHello no
longer fits one 1200-byte datagram, so it is sent as two fragments. The
first fragment is 1200 bytes and passes; the short tail is not a multiple of
four and is always dropped. The server's DTLS transport waits for the rest of
the ClientHello forever, so the connection stays in `connecting` after ICE
succeeds over a relay pair.

Allow up to three padding bytes after `len`. Upstream master still has the
strict match; drop this patch once a release accepts padding.

--- a/lib/ex_turn/client.ex
+++ b/lib/ex_turn/client.ex
@@ -403,7 +403,13 @@
     {:channel_expired, peer_addr, client}
   end
 
-  defp handle_channel_data(client, <<channel_number::16, len::16, data::binary-size(len)>>) do
+  # A server may pad ChannelData to a multiple of four bytes even over UDP
+  # (RFC 8656, section 12.5), so up to three trailing bytes follow `len`.
+  defp handle_channel_data(
+         client,
+         <<channel_number::16, len::16, data::binary-size(len), padding::binary>>
+       )
+       when byte_size(padding) <= 3 do
     case Map.get(client.channel_addr, channel_number) do
       nil ->
         Logger.debug("""
